Original commit body from 5a07938838d3d5976cb3d1edb00d8563cf42dad8:

Both tests in tests/test_check_edge_type_drift.py called cli.main()
bare. main takes argv: list[str] | None = None and falls through to
sys.argv[1:] when it is None, so under pytest argparse received
*pytest's* arguments and exited 2:

  pytest tests/ -q
  __main__.py: error: unrecognized arguments: tests/ -q

The result was a test whose verdict depended on how the suite was
invoked rather than on the code under test. Reproduced identically on a
detached worktree at origin/dev, so it predated the change that
surfaced it.

Both call sites now pass [] explicitly, verified across three
invocation shapes (file + -q, file alone, whole tests/ tree with -k).
The None default is deliberately left alone: it is correct for the real
CLI, where --strict must reach argparse. The docstring records why the
argument is explicit so it is not "simplified" back out.

Swept the class rather than assuming it was alone: three sibling tests
call a bare main() -- deleak-ledger, generate-security-md,
refresh-stdlib-modules -- and none share the defect. The first sets
sys.argv itself; the other two parse no arguments at all. Of the five
drift linters, check-edge-type-drift is the only one with an argparse
shell, which is why it is the only one exposed.

